#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "563087c2-8d87-5b95-833d-f9892d9276a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10535" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\arccos(x){:}$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)-\\pi }{x-\\pi }) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)-\\pi }{0-\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi )}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184239 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"}] :question/id #uuid "c1ba0b9f-78d6-5791-8816-b71da089e57f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10536" :question/problem "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y = \\arccos(x-1){:}$\n\n![](images/4c/4ccf7e79e19f49027f615a075e655139e6926b3ba30fe35d63078fd7917d8869.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2].$ This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/id #uuid "7ce46ca9-cfff-5e8b-89b9-a2a7b34921fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10537" :question/problem "Compute the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/06/06d01e3eb47f69e5172e0ba3c99708a05c79b12a48764fe29c50e94f93b73dd1.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph tends to $y={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55eb4c92-42d8-57d6-acd0-5c895b942670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273118} {:answer/id #uuid "7d403926-4450-50ba-84c9-7d9aa1f989d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273459} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ac37e7-92df-5ede-8595-901bef018a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273780} {:answer/id #uuid "c91605a5-ab4d-581f-9f88-55a0efff989c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274131} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0629a1-6d9e-577a-b40f-38715a13225c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d403926-4450-50ba-84c9-7d9aa1f989d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273459} :answer-field/id #uuid "94a2e6e1-9b22-5760-a9d0-b544cf1616d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "861b507b-5ca9-5f73-9449-f84c92b1d90f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152890" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5112866-46c5-505a-9ff5-4366099f77af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5112866-46c5-505a-9ff5-4366099f77af" :a