:question/math-academy-id "q-343173" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x):$ \n\n![](images/04/04dd8257ef32291b930aa29f5bed4de2c41761692fd48d08ed2479f6cbd78659.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=\\frac{7}{\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184239 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfcef8b8-e48b-586b-982b-90736aa7e6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfcef8b8-e48b-586b-982b-90736aa7e6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232897} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac9de9a4-5abd-5b97-b26f-10ae4f6b9e64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7acfea16-03cd-5fb2-a92b-f0cb7886f6f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343227" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(2x+7))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(2x+7):$\n\n![](images/37/371cbb841c67494626038fc93747a1d0a2b2245f422364911f1a859f4711868d.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(2x+7)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(2x+7)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(2x+7))=\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184239 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa252a79-6198-5339-b8cd-9b165faf3c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa252a79-6198-5339-b8cd-9b165faf3c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232921} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa58ccf0-ef3f-5aee-9f0d-b90bf4397dde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecbfd230-3cf4-56df-961c-5ee65b48f356" :question/math-academy-id "q-344536" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}(x+1))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(x+1):$ \n\n![](images/4d/4d81187377609f2cc79f2f972b1416a00f854d8c7a72731eb306aaa17d9f7339.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(x+1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}(x+1))=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=2.\n$$"}]}
